      detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\n`async def` defines a coroutine function. Calling it creates a coroutine object; its body does not simply run to completion like a normal `def` call. The coroutine runs when it is awaited or scheduled as a task. A normal `def` runs synchronously until it returns or raises, unless it delegates work elsewhere.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA normal \n\n```python\ndef\n```\n\n executes when called and returns its result directly. An \n\n```python\nasync def\n```\n\n creates a coroutine object when called; its body runs when the coroutine is awaited or scheduled as a task. That distinction is why forgetting to await an async function can produce warnings and missing work.",
